        "body": "Security leadership roles across AI companies, infrastructure operators, and adjacent sectors show sustained hiring activity with salaries ranging from $88,089 to $175,542 annually, suggesting security functions remain embedded within broader organizational structures rather than concentrated in pure AI firms.\n\nThe distribution of postings reveals two distinct hiring patterns. Specialized AI companies like OpenAI, Cohere, and Nebius are hiring security roles focused on travel, site operations, and reliability engineering, reflecting the physical footprint and supply chain complexity required by infrastructure-heavy AI operations. Meanwhile, traditional enterprise and government contractors are posting security leadership roles at comparable or higher compensation, indicating that demand for these positions extends far beyond the AI sector itself.\n\nThe geographic spread and role diversity underscore that security at scale is inseparable from the buildings, supply chains, and geographic distribution that power AI compute. Site security managers, transportation security roles, and regional supply chain positions appear alongside traditional cybersecurity titles. The data does not reveal hiring velocity, whether these represent net growth or replacement cycles, or how security headcount correlates to compute capacity or data center expansion. It shows only that organizations operating large physical and digital infrastructure are actively recruiting security leadership.",
